# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# openclaw INBOUND WEBHOOK ROUTES — v4.1 Phase 1c (LAURA-I1-03 / FP-05)
# Last updated: 2026-07-06
#
# SINGLE SOURCE. Defines the (openclaw-webhooks) named snippet. Imported
# (top-level) by every Caddyfile variant
#   docker/Caddyfile.{selfsigned,ca,acme}  via  import /etc/caddy/Caddyfile.openclaw-webhooks
# and pulled into each variant's public :443 site block with
#   import openclaw-webhooks
# The Helm caddy ConfigMap (helm/yashigani/templates/configmaps.yaml) carries
# the hand-coded K8s render of the same routes (yashigani-* service names),
# gated on .Values.agentBundles.openclaw.enabled.
#
# WHY: with openclaw off the `edge` bridge (LAURA-I1-03 close), Slack/Telegram
# webhooks can no longer reach openclaw:18789 directly (it was host-loopback
# published; that publish is removed — internal-only bridges cannot DNAT).
# Inbound now arrives EXCLUSIVELY through these Caddy routes:
#   internet → caddy:443 /webhooks/{slack,telegram} → forward_auth verify
#            → openclaw:18789 over the dedicated openclaw_ringfence bridge.
#
# FP-05 hardening (Laura threat model, all mandatory):
#   * EXACT POST paths only — /webhooks/slack and /webhooks/telegram. The
#     /webhooks/* fallthrough 404s everything else. openclaw's full API
#     surface (health/admin/config on :18789) is NEVER published; there is
#     deliberately no `reverse_proxy /*` to openclaw anywhere.
#   * Fail-closed signature verification BEFORE openclaw sees the request:
#     forward_auth → backoffice /auth/verify-webhook (mirrors the
#     /auth/verify-mcp pattern, c3b1a0e6). forward_auth denies on ANY non-2xx
#     from the verifier — backoffice down / endpoint missing / Redis down all
#     read as DENY at the edge.
#   * Replay dedupe + rate-limit live in the verifier (stock caddy:2.11.2 has
#     no rate_limit module — same design decision as the Layer-B HMAC
#     fallback, 2026-04-29: no custom Caddy build).
#   * Body cap 1MB per route (Slack events + Telegram updates are KB-scale;
#     openclaw mem_limit 1024m — unbounded POST would be API4:2023 DoS).
#   * SSRF-back (FP-05/FP-07): URLs carried in webhook payloads (Slack
#     response_url, Telegram file paths) are fetched by openclaw via the
#     :18790 egress gateway ONLY — openclaw has no direct internet route at
#     L3, so the inbound route cannot be used to trigger unfiltered outbound.
#
# ── CONTRACT — backoffice GET /auth/verify-webhook (Tom follow-up) ──────────
# Caddy's forward_auth issues a GET subrequest with the ORIGINAL HEADERS but
# NO BODY (Caddy strips the body on forward_auth subrequests — this is
# documented Caddy behaviour and shapes the verification split below).
#
#   Request (from Caddy, over internal mTLS + Layer-B HMAC header):
#     GET /auth/verify-webhook?provider={slack|telegram}
#     X-Forwarded-Method: <original method>     (set by forward_auth)
#     X-Forwarded-Uri:    <original URI>        (set by forward_auth)
#     X-Caddy-Verified-Secret: <layer-B HMAC>   (inject-caddy-verified)
#     + all original client headers (Slack/Telegram signature headers included)
#
#   MUST enforce, fail-closed (any failure → 401; store down → 503; never 2xx
#   on a downgrade path — release-gate SOP 4):
#     0. CaddyVerifiedMiddleware Layer-B check (present for every backoffice
#        route already) — a direct public call to /auth/verify-webhook without
#        the per-install secret is 401 by construction.
#     1. X-Forwarded-Method == POST.
#     2. provider == slack:
#          a. X-Slack-Request-Timestamp present, integer, |now - ts| <= 300s.
#          b. X-Slack-Signature present, format v0=<64 hex>.
#          c. Replay dedupe: Redis SETNX sha256(X-Slack-Signature) TTL 600s;
#             duplicate → 401. (The signature is a deterministic MAC over
#             v0:ts:body, so an exact replay is detectable WITHOUT the body.)
#        provider == telegram:
#          a. X-Telegram-Bot-Api-Secret-Token present; constant-time compare
#             (hmac.compare_digest) against the per-install secret at
#             /run/secrets/openclaw_telegram_webhook_secret (install.sh mint;
#             operator supplies the same value to Telegram setWebhook).
#     3. Rate-limit: per-IP (X-Real-IP) and global buckets in Redis;
#        exceeded → 429 (Caddy copies it to the client — still a deny).
#     4. Audit every deny on the tamper-evident chain
#        (WEBHOOK_INGRESS_DENIED event type, mirroring MCP_INGRESS_DENIED).
#     5. Unknown/missing provider param → 401.
#
#   Body-MAC layer split (documented per Laura FP-05 "document which"):
#   the Slack HMAC is computed over v0:ts:BODY; the verifier subrequest has no
#   body, so full HMAC verification happens at the body-holding hop — openclaw's
#   Slack channel integration verifies the signing secret before any
#   side-effect (standard Slack SDK behaviour; openclaw is configured with the
#   signing secret). The verifier enforces freshness + exact-replay dedupe +
#   rate + shape at the edge. Telegram verification (header secret_token) is
#   COMPLETE at the verifier. Laura Phase-3 test (d) proves the end-to-end
#   drop; if openclaw's Slack verification cannot be evidenced, the escalation
#   is a Tom verify-AND-RELAY endpoint (full body), never a weaker gate.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
(openclaw-webhooks) {
    # ── Slack events / slash-commands / interactivity ───────────────────────
    handle /webhooks/slack {
        route {
            @not_post not method POST
            respond @not_post "Method Not Allowed" 405
            request_body {
                max_size 1MB
            }
            forward_auth https://backoffice:8443 {
            import internal-mtls
            import inject-caddy-verified
                uri /auth/verify-webhook?provider=slack
            }
            reverse_proxy http://openclaw:18789 {
                header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
                header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
            }
        }
    }

    # ── Telegram bot updates ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    handle /webhooks/telegram {
        route {
            @not_post not method POST
            respond @not_post "Method Not Allowed" 405
            request_body {
                max_size 1MB
            }
            forward_auth https://backoffice:8443 {
            import internal-mtls
            import inject-caddy-verified
                uri /auth/verify-webhook?provider=telegram
            }
            reverse_proxy http://openclaw:18789 {
                header_up X-Forwarded-Proto https
                header_up X-Real-IP {remote_host}
            }
        }
    }

    # Default-deny for the webhook namespace — nothing else under /webhooks/
    # exists, and openclaw's wider :18789 surface is never published.
    handle /webhooks/* {
        respond "Not Found" 404
    }
}
